krauken (0.1.8) stable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: identify_relay_pin's bare uninstall of whatever currently held
    CHAMBER_HEAT never turned the pin off -- confirmed live against the
    real rig: DeviceManager.cpp's uninstallDevice() only deletes the C++
    actuator object, it never calls setActive(false) first, so a pin
    that had genuinely engaged (e.g. the identified cooling relay) stayed
    electrically on indefinitely after being abandoned for the next
    candidate. New _safe_uninstall_heat helper checks whether the current
    holder is genuinely engaged right now; if so, it reinstalls the same
    pin with inverted polarity first (the still-active heat demand
    re-engages it near-instantly under the flip, driving the pin to the
    correct physical off level for its real wiring), confirms that
    re-engagement, then uninstalls for real. A candidate that was only
    ever waiting or timed out with no engagement still gets a bare
    uninstall, unchanged.
  * Fix: HardwareWizard.tsx's devicePinSweep offered a "Skip -- try the
    next test" button any time a candidate was waiting on the
    compressor-protection timer, with no regard for whether this sweep
    had ever actually engaged before -- confirmed live: skipping before
    the sweep's first genuine engagement doesn't save time, it discards
    the only real ~600s wait BrewPi's anti-short-cycle timer requires and
    forces the next candidate to pay it again from scratch, silently.
    New everEngaged state latches the first time any candidate reaches a
    real engagement; until then, no Skip button is offered at all, just
    an explanation that this first wait only happens once. Confirmed live
    this session: every subsequent candidate swap after the first real
    engagement (~2m15s) went to HEATING near-instantly, five times in a
    row, across two relays and both polarities.

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Mon, 17 Aug 2026 20:40:57 -0400

krauken (0.1.7) stable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: platforms/brewpi/device_config.py's pin-sweep (v0.1.6's
    sweep_relay) is redesigned entirely -- found live, against the real
    rig, that testing cooling and heating as two separate passes (each
    forcing its own target direction) left stray same-function candidates
    installed between passes, and BrewPi does *not* enforce
    one-actuator-per-function on its own: commanding "cooling" energized
    three separate installed CHAMBER_COOL devices at once, not just the
    intended one. Renamed to identify_relay_pin and rebuilt around a
    single invariant -- at most one device is ever installed with the
    CHAMBER_HEAT function at a time, uninstalling whatever currently
    holds it before installing the next candidate -- and a single
    mechanism: only ever force a target *above* the current chamber temp
    (never a cooling demand), and let the human observer say what
    physically responded (heater, fridge, or nothing), since the
    firmware's function label doesn't need to match physical reality for
    the test to work. Normal and reversed polarity are now two automatic
    sub-tests per candidate, never a user-facing choice.
  * Fix: the pin-sweep could never succeed at all as previously
    sequenced -- it needs a live chamber-temperature reading to compute a
    baseline, but nothing was ever installed with the CHAMBER_TEMP
    function until the very last wizard step (finalize), which runs
    *after* the sweep. New install_probe action installs the identified
    chamber (and beer) probe immediately once confirmed in the wizard,
    not deferred to finalize.
  * Fix: identify_onewire_probes gave up after a fixed 90s and forced a
    manual "Try again" click, even though there's no hardware reason
    (unlike the relay sweep's real anti-short-cycle window) for this step
    to be time-boxed. It now polls until identified or the user cancels
    setup, with only a 3600s backstop against a truly orphaned task.
  * New: begin_device_config -- the wizard's first action -- snapshots
    the current installed-device state before wiping every device to
    unassigned, so a candidate pin is always tested from a genuinely
    blank slate. If a previous wizard session was abandoned mid-sweep
    (tab closed, no cancel), the *next* session self-heals by restoring
    that leftover snapshot first. reset_brewpi's cancel path now restores
    the same snapshot rather than just resetting -- a real "revert to the
    beginning state."
  * Fix: HardwareWizard.tsx's device-config stages could flash a false
    "no probe detected" for one render on every job-to-job transition
    (TanStack Query resets `data` to undefined the instant the polled
    test_id changes, before the new job's first response arrives) --
    confirmed live, reproduced on both the initial probe-identification
    entry and the chamber-to-beer phase transition. A genuinely failed
    sweep job (e.g. the chamber probe isn't reading) was also silently
    rendered as an ordinary "no cooling/heating seen" timeout, hiding the
    real error and inviting repeated identical failures across every
    remaining candidate -- now surfaced explicitly with the real error
    and a Retry action.
  * Fix: krauken/ipc/server.py's stop() hung for a full 90s
    (systemd's TimeoutStopSec) and got SIGKILLed on every
    krauken-manual/krauken-simulator restart, confirmed via real
    install timing -- Python 3.12 changed Server.wait_closed() to also
    wait for already-open client connections (e.g. the daemon's own
    persistent connection to these services), which never close on
    their own once idle. IPCServer now tracks and aborts open
    connections itself before waiting, restoring the pre-3.12 shutdown
    behavior.
  * Improved: krauken.postinst now echoes progress through each real
    step (venv creation, dependency install/skip, demo-seed trigger,
    eeptools build, service restart) -- the slow dependency-install step
    in particular had zero visible progress from apt/dpkg's own display,
    which looked identical to a hung install on a real Pi Zero W.

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 13:12:52 -0400

krauken (0.1.6) stable; urgency=medium

  * New: Krauken now takes over BrewPi's own classic Device
    Configuration page entirely -- a guided wizard discovers which
    OneWire probe is chamber/beer and which pin drives cooling (and
    optional heating), then installs that mapping itself, so a user
    never needs BrewPi's separate web UI again. Replaces the previous
    confirm_heater/identify_probes-based setup flow, which silently
    assumed that mapping already existed via BrewPi's own UI -- an
    assumption confirmed false on the actual reference rig (a cooling
    actuator installed, no heat device installed anywhere). Everything
    below was verified against the real Arduino (firmware 0.2.13) and
    brewpi-remix/brewpi-firmware-rmx's actual source, not guessed.
  * New: platforms/brewpi/connection.py gains list_installed_devices/
    list_available_devices/list_all_devices/install_device/
    reset_and_reconnect, backed by the Arduino's 'd'/'h'/'U'/'R'
    commands. Installing a device requires an explicit, currently-
    unused slot number (0-15) -- confirmed via DeviceManager.cpp's
    source and a live write against real hardware that -1 (or omitting
    the slot) fails silently. A real firmware quirk found this session
    (an unrelated log message sometimes interleaves mid-array in a
    'd'/'h' response, splitting one JSON array across physical lines)
    is now handled explicitly rather than silently failing to parse.
  * New: platforms/brewpi/device_config.py's guided pin-sweep pays the
    real anti-short-cycle wait (up to 10 minutes, confirmed via
    TempControl.h's MIN_SWITCH_TIME/MIN_COOL_OFF_TIME_FRIDGE_CONSTANT)
    only once per session -- once any relay has genuinely engaged,
    reassigning the tested function to a different candidate pin
    engages the new one within one control-loop tick, since the
    firmware's own protection timers track the logical function, not
    which pin backs it (confirmed via DeviceManager.cpp). A positively-
    identified relay is deliberately left energized while the sweep
    continues for the other function -- the protection timers guard
    against rapid cycling, not continuous running -- and the final
    pushed configuration is followed by a real hardware reset, which
    deterministically forces every pin to a safe level via the
    firmware's own actuator-construction ordering.
  * New: platforms/registry.py's PlatformBinding gains a test_runners
    table (via the new PlatformTestRunner in contracts/interfaces.py)
    so a platform can own hardware-configuration test actions that
    don't fit the existing ChamberDriver/BeerTempSource/GravitySource
    Protocols, without teaching daemon/tests_runtime.py any
    platform-specific knowledge. confirm_heater/identify_probes/
    fire_outlet are unchanged -- they were already fully generic.
  * Fix: api/schemas.py's TestStartRequest.action was missing
    "confirm_heater" entirely -- a real API call to start it was
    rejected before ever reaching the daemon. Also adds the five new
    device-configuration actions.
  * Kept: confirm_heater survives as a standalone, narrower diagnostic
    (HardwareSetupView's "Test heater" button, shown once heating is
    already mapped) for "did my already-configured heater stop
    responding" re-verification -- it no longer drives initial setup.

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Sun, 16 Aug 2026 07:52:44 -0400

krauken (0.1.5) stable; urgency=high

  * Fix: platforms/simulator/plant.py's gravity_at() raised
    OverflowError ("math range error") for a sufficiently large
    positive elapsed-time input -- unlike every other math.exp() call
    in this module, whose exponents are unconditionally <= 0 by
    construction (see advance_physics's own docstring on a prior,
    similar incident), gravity_at()'s plain logistic formula needed a
    genuinely positive exponent past its midpoint, which overflows.
    Caught live on real hardware: a remapped-to-pure-simulator active
    fermentation, after a daemon restart onto the accelerated
    SimulatorClock, hit this on essentially the first control tick,
    and the control loop's own "one bad tick must never kill control"
    resilience then retried the identical failing read forever --
    with SimulatorClock never actually waiting between ticks, this
    pinned the Pi's single CPU core and produced >100 log lines/second
    indefinitely. Fixed with the standard numerically-stable sigmoid
    rewrite (only ever exponentiate the non-positive half, whichever
    side of the midpoint t_h is on) -- verified identical output to the
    original formula for every normal input, and that extreme t_h now
    correctly saturates at the curve's own asymptotes instead of
    raising. New tests/unit/test_simulator_plant.py (plant.py had no
    direct unit coverage at all before this).

 -- The Krauken Project <dneumann@arpio.io>  Sat, 15 Aug 2026 16:39:30 -0400

krauken (0.1.4) stable; urgency=medium

  * Fix: postinst re-ran the full `pip install -e '.[pi]'` on every
    single upgrade, even completely no-op ones -- measured on a real
    Raspberry Pi Zero W (single 1GHz ARM11 core): ~50s of CPU time just
    to unpack a single already-cached, piwheels-prebuilt wheel
    (pydantic-core), so re-resolving the whole dependency set on every
    upgrade was real, needless minutes of install time. Now skipped
    entirely unless pyproject.toml has actually changed since the last
    successful install (tracked via a stored hash) -- editable installs
    already pick up plain code changes for free.
  * New: demo fermentation data (krauken-db seed-demo, already existed
    as a CLI command but was never wired into the install) now seeds
    automatically on a genuinely fresh install (keyed off whether the
    real db file exists yet, never on upgrade). Backgrounded via a new
    one-shot krauken-seed-demo.service, not installed via
    dh_installsystemd and never auto-(re)started, so `apt install`
    itself isn't blocked on a real compressed fermentation run -- real,
    measurable minutes of work on hardware this weak.

